    "case_id": "645",
    "case_summary": "A 60-year-old woman presented with recurrent intense epigastric pain for 4-5 days. Episodes consisted of sudden burning pain in the epigastrium lasting 10-50 minutes, followed by nausea without emesis, and preceded by visual and auditory hallucinations. These episodes began 1 month after experiencing a severe COVID-19 infection that required ICU stay without respiratory support. Medical history included granulomatosis with polyangiitis diagnosed 3 years prior, stage 3 chronic kidney disease, and hypertension.\n\nAbdominal physical examination was unremarkable.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:    * Normal cardiac biomarkers, pancreatic enzymes, and liver function tests    * Normal blood count without anemia    * Normal procalcitonin level    * Detailed lab values as per Table 1 showing minor deviations in glucose, creatinine, uric acid, albumin, leukocytes, and neutrophils  - Imaging Studies:    * Electrocardiogram      Title: Figure 1      Description: Shows sinus tachycardia only    * Esophagogastroduodenoscopy      Description: Revealed mild gastritis    * Mesenteric artery tomography angiography      Description: Normal findings    * Quantitative Electroencephalogram      Title: Figure 2      Description: Shows epileptiform activity in the right temporo-occipital region    * Magnetic Resonance Imaging      Title: Figure 3      Description: T2 sequence showing hyperintense punctate images in the white matter typical of a vascular pattern; no findings in temporal or occipital lobe",
